# HyperI Auditd HIGH profile - for customer sites
# License:      Apache-2.0
# Copyright:    (c) 2026 HYPERI PTY LIMITED
#
# HISTORY. Forked from Neo23x0 (Florian Roth) "Best Practice Auditd
# Configuration" (Apache-2.0), then progressively merged with the public
# baselines below. Method, and it matters when reading the changelog: each merge
# took the DELTA the newer source had that we did not, and replaced our line
# where the newer source did it better. Roughly every second merge was followed
# by a performance review, because breadth accrues and CPU does not.
#   Neo23x0 (Florian Roth) -> ACSC (ASD) OpenSCAP -> NIST -> DISA STIG
#   -> alphagov/GDS -> US DoD -> SigmaHQ
# Detection keys carry MITRE ATT&CK technique IDs where mapped.
#
# SIGMA ALIGNMENT. Our events are consumed by a pipeline that auto-ingests
# SigmaHQ rules, so a rule here exists partly to make a Sigma rule FIREABLE: a
# Sigma auditd rule whose event we never emit is a dead detection - it loads,
# looks like coverage, and never matches. Rules marked "sigma:" below exist for
# that reason. Detection logic taken from SigmaHQ (https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma),
# Detection Rule License 1.1 (https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License).
#
# TRAP - DO NOT APPEND A SYSCALL TO AN EXISTING "-S" LIST. An unknown syscall
# name makes auditctl drop the WHOLE rule, not just that name (auditctl.c: the
# parse returns -1 and the rule is never sent to the kernel). "-i" stops the LOAD
# aborting; it does NOT rescue the rule. Appending a modern syscall to a working
# -S list therefore SILENTLY deletes that rule's entire coverage on el6/el7 -
# and -i, the thing that makes our old-distro support work, is what hides it.
# Every new syscall goes on its OWN line.
#
# DO-NOT-MERGE from upstream linux-audit: 10-base-config.rules still ships
# --backlog_wait_time 60000, the exact host-CPU bug v3.0 fixed, and
# 20-dont-audit.rules ships its noise suppressors commented out. Treating that
# file as "the newer source" would reintroduce the throttle.
#
# Profile: HIGH (selected via hyperi_audit_level=high). MEDIUM plus the heavier
# breadth: per-user + root interpreter exec, the full setuid/DAC syscall
# family, IPC, recon toolset, data-staging, session/PAM, and third-party
# agents. For high-value hosts that can carry the extra volume/CPU and the
# extra forwarding bandwidth. NOT for constrained or slow-link endpoints.
#
# Changelog (newest first). Entries below v3.0 were rebuilt from the merge order
# when the pre-git tracking was retired.
# v3.3   2026-07-20  Baseline refresh from ACSC/NIST/DISA STIG: AppArmor MAC
#                    tamper (Ubuntu was a whole-distro blind spot), nsswitch,
#                    legacy pam.conf, gpasswd/chage, fchmodat2 (own line -
#                    modern-kernel chmod), open_by_handle_at (own line - Shocker
#                    container escape). The hyperi_audit_use_case role variable
#                    was renamed hyperi_audit_profile_override.
# v3.2   2026-07-17  Sigma alignment + baseline refresh. Added the rare,
#                    high-signal rules that make otherwise-dead SigmaHQ rules
#                    fireable, plus the Neo23x0 2026-05 socket delta.
#                    Fixed: user crontabs were watched on the Debian path
#                    only, so cron persistence went unlogged on the EL matrix.
#                    Fixed: T1548.001_priv_ctx_exec fired on EVERY daemon
#                    execve (auid=unset is not != uid).
# v3.1   2026-07-17  Escalated-execution rules moved ABOVE the excludes: the
#                    excludes match auid>=1000 + path with no euid scoping, so
#                    below them `sudo cat /tmp/x` went unlogged.
# v3.0   2026-07-16  Host-CPU fix + modernisation (see hyperi-audit-medium.rules).
#                    --backlog_wait_time 0; -i first; new kernel TTPs; dangling
#                    -F fix; HIGH breadth restored on the modern base.
# v2.9   2025-01-02  MITRE TIDs; non-root high-frequency execve excludes.
#                    Customer-site perf review.
# v2.7   2024-11-07  Tiered auditd migrated from PaaS (high profile).
# v2.6   2023-11-08  MITRE merge; virtualisation.
# v2.5   2023-06-14  US DoD merge.
# v2.4   2023-03-21  alphagov/GDS (UK Government Digital Service) merge, via
#                    Neo23x0. Recorded as "GCHQ" until 2026-07-17.
# v2.3   2022-11-01  Updates, merges and fixes.
# v2.2.1 2022-03-08  Customer-site perf review + merge.
# v2.2   2022-01-10  HyperI infra merge.
# v1.7   2021-09-03  DISA STIG merge.
# v1.6   2021-06-23  NIST merge. Tweaks and fixes.
# v1.5   2021-06-01  ACSC OpenSCAP merge.
# v1.4   2021-01-11  Initial fork from Neo23x0.
#
# Assumes a 64-bit host (see the 32-bit ABI tripwire). Events are forwarded
# off-box via rsyslog imfile reading /var/log/audit/audit.log, NOT audisp.

# --- Service level configuration -------------------------------------------

## Remove any existing rules
-D

## Ignore-errors FIRST: unknown syscalls/options on older kernels (el6) are
## skipped rather than aborting the whole ruleset load.
-i

## Kernel backlog buffer. HIGH generates more events - keep headroom so
## wait_time=0 drops as little as possible on busy el7+ hosts.
-b 16384

## HOST-CPU FIX: never let the kernel block a syscall waiting for the audit
## backlog to drain. 0 = drop on overflow instead of throttling the host.
## Requires kernel >=3.14; el6 skips this line via -i.
--backlog_wait_time 0

## Make loginuid immutable (anti-tamper on auid). Kernel >=3.4; el6 skips.
--loginuid-immutable

## Failure mode: 1 = printk (0 silent, 2 panic).
-f 1

# --- Escalated execution (euid=0) - MUST stay ABOVE the excludes -----------
## Order matters: the exit filter is first-match-wins and the excludes below
## match on auid+path with no euid scoping. Below them, `sudo cat /tmp/x`
## goes unlogged - auid stays the login uid, so the exclude wins.
## Do not scope the excludes by euid instead: auid=unset (4294967295) is
## >=1000, so that would un-exclude every daemon and flood the backlog.
## DO NOT move the excludes above this block.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F euid=0 -F dir=/home          -k T1059_root_home
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F euid=0 -F dir=/tmp           -k T1059_root_tmp
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F euid=0 -F dir=/var/tmp       -k T1059_root_vartmp
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F euid=0 -F dir=/dev/shm       -k T1059_root_devshm
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F euid=0 -F dir=/usr/local/bin -k T1059_root_localbin
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F euid=0 -F dir=/opt           -k T1059_root_opt
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F euid=0 -F sgid!=0 -k T1059_setgid_exec
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F euid=0 -F suid!=0 -k T1059_setuid_exec

# --- Web-server-user execution (euid=33) - MUST stay ABOVE the excludes ----
## Web-server-user process execution - the classic webshell/RCE signal, and a
## high-value host is often web-facing too. euid=33 is www-data on
## Debian/Ubuntu; adjust per host (apache/nginx often 48 or 996 on EL).
## Placement, same first-match-wins reason as the euid=0 block above: a
## webshell's first move is cat/ls/grep/whoami and the excludes below match
## exactly those paths on auid>=1000. www-data runs with auid=unset
## (4294967295), which IS >=1000, so below the excludes this rule would be
## dead for precisely the binaries a webshell reaches for first.
## sigma: syscall/lnx_auditd_web_rce.yml
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F euid=33 -k T1505.003_webshell
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execveat -F euid=33 -k T1505.003_webshell

# --- Exclude high-frequency, low-risk records (efficiency) -----------------
## Cheap excludes, kept early - but BELOW the escalated-execution block above.
-a never,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F auid>=1000 -F path=/usr/bin/grep   -F perm=x -k T1059_exclude
-a never,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F auid>=1000 -F path=/usr/bin/sleep  -F perm=x -k T1059_exclude
-a never,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F auid>=1000 -F path=/bin/ping       -F perm=x -k T1059_exclude
-a never,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F auid>=1000 -F path=/bin/ls         -F perm=x -k T1059_exclude
-a never,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F auid>=1000 -F path=/bin/cat        -F perm=x -k T1059_exclude
-a never,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F auid>=1000 -F path=/usr/bin/less   -F perm=x -k T1059_exclude
-a never,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F auid>=1000 -F path=/usr/bin/more   -F perm=x -k T1059_exclude
-a never,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F auid>=1000 -F path=/usr/bin/head   -F perm=x -k T1059_exclude
-a never,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F auid>=1000 -F path=/usr/bin/tail   -F perm=x -k T1059_exclude
-a never,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F auid>=1000 -F path=/usr/bin/which  -F perm=x -k T1059_exclude
-a never,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F auid>=1000 -F path=/usr/bin/whoami -F perm=x -k T1059_exclude
-a never,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F auid>=1000 -F path=/usr/bin/sort   -F perm=x -k T1059_exclude

## Record-type noise (each msgtype needs its own line - cannot be combined)
-a always,exclude -F msgtype=CWD -k exclude_cwd
-a always,exclude -F msgtype=EOE -k exclude_eoe
-a always,exclude -F msgtype=CRYPTO_KEY_USER -k exclude_crypto_user
## SELinux AVC denials are LEFT LOGGED (they flag exploit attempts). Uncomment
## on SELinux-enforcing hosts only if AVC volume becomes a problem:
# -a always,exclude -F msgtype=AVC -k exclude_avc

## Daemon self-noise
-a never,user -F subj_type=crond_t -k T1053_cron_ignore
-a never,exit -F subj_type=crond_t -k T1053_cron_ignore
-a never,exit -F arch=b64 -S adjtimex -F auid=unset -F uid=chrony -F subj_type=chronyd_t -k T1099_chrony_ignore
-a never,exit -F arch=b64 -S fork -F success=0 -F path=/usr/lib/vmware-tools -F subj_type=initrc_t -F exit=-2 -k T1569_vmwaretools_ignore
-a never,exit -F arch=b64 -S fork -F success=0 -F path=/etc/vmware-tools -F subj_type=initrc_t -F exit=-2 -k T1569_vmwaretools_ignore
-a never,exit -F arch=b64 -S all -F exe=/usr/bin/vmtoolsd -k T1569_vmwaretools_ignore
-a never,exit -F arch=b64 -F dir=/dev/shm -k T1135_sharedmem_ignore
-a never,exit -F arch=b64 -F dir=/var/lock/lvm -k exclude_locklvm
## Kernel tracing pseudo-filesystems (filesystem filter: newer kernels; el6/el7 skip)
-a never,filesystem -F fstype=tracefs -k exclude_tracefs
-a never,filesystem -F fstype=debugfs -k exclude_debugfs

# --- Self auditing ---------------------------------------------------------
-w /var/log/audit/ -p wra -k T1005_auditlog
-w /var/audit/     -p wra -k T1005_auditlog
-w /etc/audit/     -p wa  -k auditconfig
-w /etc/libaudit.conf -p wa -k auditconfig
-w /etc/audisp/    -p wa  -k audispconfig
-w /sbin/auditctl  -p x   -k audittools
-w /sbin/auditd    -p x   -k audittools
-w /usr/sbin/auditd -p x  -k audittools
-w /usr/sbin/augenrules -p x -k audittools
-a always,exit -F path=/usr/sbin/ausearch    -F perm=x -k T1005_auditlog
-a always,exit -F path=/usr/sbin/aureport    -F perm=x -k T1005_auditlog
-a always,exit -F path=/usr/sbin/aulast      -F perm=x -k T1005_auditlog
-a always,exit -F path=/usr/sbin/aulastlogin -F perm=x -k T1005_auditlog
-a always,exit -F path=/usr/sbin/auvirt      -F perm=x -k T1005_auditlog

# --- 32-bit ABI tripwire (assumes a 64-bit host) ---------------------------
-a always,exit -F arch=b32 -S all -F key=32bit-abi

# --- Modern kernel-level TTPs (newer kernels; el6 skips via -i) -------------
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S bpf -k T1055_ebpf
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S setns -S unshare -k T1611_namespace
## open_by_handle_at - the Shocker container escape. Its OWN line (never folded
## into an -S list): it resolves a file by handle and bypasses paths entirely,
## so no path/dir-scoped rule can catch it.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S open_by_handle_at -F auid>=1000 -F auid!=-1 -k T1611_shocker
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S io_uring_setup -k T1562_io_uring
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S memfd_create -k T1620_memfd

# --- Kernel modules, time, cron, at, systemd persistence -------------------
-w /etc/sysctl.conf -p wa -k T1547_sysctl
-w /etc/sysctl.d    -p wa -k T1547_sysctl
-a always,exit -F perm=x -F auid!=-1 -F path=/sbin/insmod   -k T1547.006_mod
-a always,exit -F perm=x -F auid!=-1 -F path=/sbin/modprobe -k T1547.006_mod
-a always,exit -F perm=x -F auid!=-1 -F path=/sbin/rmmod    -k T1547.006_mod
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S finit_module -S init_module -S delete_module -F auid!=-1 -k T1547.006_mod
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S kexec_load -k T1014_kexec
-w /etc/modprobe.conf -p wa -k T1547.006_mod
-w /etc/modprobe.d    -p wa -k T1547.006_mod
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S adjtimex -S settimeofday -S clock_settime -k T1070.006_time
-w /etc/localtime -p wa -k T1070.006_time
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S utimes -S utimensat -k T1070.006_time
-w /etc/cron.allow -p wa -k T1053.003_cron
-w /etc/cron.deny  -p wa -k T1053.003_cron
-w /etc/cron.d/       -p wa -k T1053.003_cron
-w /etc/cron.daily/   -p wa -k T1053.003_cron
-w /etc/cron.hourly/  -p wa -k T1053.003_cron
-w /etc/cron.monthly/ -p wa -k T1053.003_cron
-w /etc/cron.weekly/  -p wa -k T1053.003_cron
-w /etc/crontab       -p wa -k T1053.003_cron
## User crontabs. BOTH paths are needed and each is a no-op on the other family:
## Debian/Ubuntu use /var/spool/cron/crontabs/, RHEL/CentOS/Alma use
## /var/spool/cron/ directly. Watch the binary too: the spool file is the
## artefact, crontab(1) is the actor.
-w /var/spool/cron/crontabs/ -p wa -k T1053.003_cron
-w /var/spool/cron/          -p wa -k T1053.003_cron
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -F path=/usr/bin/crontab -F perm=x -F auid>=1000 -F auid!=unset -k T1053.003_cron
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -F path=/usr/bin/at      -F perm=x -F auid>=1000 -F auid!=unset -k T1053.002_at
-w /etc/inittab -p wa -k T1037_init
-w /etc/init.d/ -p wa -k T1037_init
-w /etc/init/   -p wa -k T1037_init
-w /sbin/init   -k T1037_init
-w /etc/at.allow -p wa -k T1053.002_at
-w /etc/at.deny  -p wa -k T1053.002_at
-w /var/spool/at/ -p wa -k T1053.002_at
-w /etc/anacrontab -p wa -k T1053.003_cron
-w /etc/systemd/system/     -k T1543.002_systemd
-w /usr/lib/systemd/system/ -k T1543.002_systemd
-w /run/systemd/system/     -k T1543.002_systemd
-w /bin/systemctl -p x -k T1543.002_systemd
-w /etc/systemd/  -p wa -k T1543.002_systemd
-w /usr/lib/systemd -p wa -k T1543.002_systemd

# --- Accounts, sudoers, credentials ----------------------------------------
-w /etc/sudoers   -p wa -k T1078.003_sudoers
-w /etc/sudoers.d -p wa -k T1078.003_sudoers
-w /usr/bin/passwd -p x -k T1078.003_passwd
-w /usr/sbin/groupadd -p x -k T1136_account
-w /usr/sbin/groupmod -p x -k T1136_account
-w /usr/sbin/addgroup -p x -k T1136_account
-w /usr/sbin/useradd  -p x -k T1136_account
-w /usr/sbin/userdel  -p x -k T1136_account
-w /usr/sbin/usermod  -p x -k T1136_account
-w /usr/sbin/adduser  -p x -k T1136_account
## gpasswd (group membership) and chage (password ageing) - the account-manip
## siblings the useradd/usermod/group* watches above miss. auid-scoped so daemon
## churn stays out.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -F path=/usr/bin/gpasswd -F perm=x -F auid>=1000 -F auid!=unset -k T1136_account
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -F path=/usr/bin/chage   -F perm=x -F auid>=1000 -F auid!=unset -k T1098_account
-w /etc/group   -p wa -k T1087.001_localgroup
-w /etc/passwd  -p wa -k T1087.001_localuser
-w /etc/gshadow -k T1087.001_shadow
-w /etc/shadow  -p r -k T1087.001_shadow
-w /etc/security/opasswd -k T1087.001_opasswd
-w /usr/bin/ldapsearch -p x -k T1087.002_ldap
-a always,exit -S setresuid -F a0=0 -F exe=/usr/bin/sudo -k T1548.003_sudo
-a always,exit -F dir=/home -F uid=0 -F auid>=1000 -F auid!=-1 -C auid!=obj_uid -k T1548.003_power_abuse
-w /bin/su -p x -k T1548.003_su
-w /usr/bin/sudo -p x -k T1548.003_sudo
-w /usr/bin/pkexec -p x -k T1068_CVE_2021_4034
-w /usr/bin/dbus-send -p x -k T1068_CVE_2021_3560
-w /usr/bin/gdbus -p x -k T1068_CVE_2021_3560

# --- DAC / permission modifications (attributable) -------------------------
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S chmod -S fchmod -S fchmodat -F auid>=1000 -F auid!=-1 -k T1222_perm_mod
## fchmodat2 (kernel 6.6+) is the new routing target for chmod - the -S list
## above never sees it. Its OWN line, NOT appended to that list (an unknown
## syscall name would drop the whole perm-mod rule on el6/el7).
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S fchmodat2 -F auid>=1000 -F auid!=-1 -k T1222_perm_mod
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S chown -S fchown -S fchownat -S lchown -F auid>=1000 -F auid!=-1 -k T1222_perm_mod
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S setxattr -S lsetxattr -S fsetxattr -S removexattr -S lremovexattr -S fremovexattr -F auid>=1000 -F auid!=-1 -k T1222_perm_mod

# --- Code injection & persistence ------------------------------------------
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S ptrace -k T1055.008_ptrace
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S ptrace -F a0=0x4 -k T1055.008_ptrace
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S ptrace -F a0=0x5 -k T1055.008_ptrace
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S ptrace -F a0=0x6 -k T1055.008_ptrace
-w /etc/ld.so.preload -k T1574.006_ldpreload
-w /etc/ld.so.conf   -p wa -k T1574.006_ldpath
-w /etc/ld.so.conf.d -p wa -k T1574.006_ldpath
-a always,exit -F dir=/etc/profile.d -F perm=w -k T1546.004_shellprofile
-w /etc/profile.d/ -k T1546.004_shellprofile
-w /etc/profile    -k T1546.004_shellprofile
-w /etc/shells     -k T1546.004_shellprofile
-w /etc/bashrc     -k T1546.004_shellprofile
-w /etc/csh.cshrc  -k T1546.004_shellprofile
-w /etc/csh.login  -k T1546.004_shellprofile
-w /root/.bash_profile -p w -k T1546.004_rootbash
-w /root/.bashrc       -p w -k T1546.004_rootbash
-w /etc/ssh/sshd_config   -p wa -k T1098.004_sshd
-w /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d -p wa -k T1098.004_sshd
-w /root/.ssh -p wa -k T1098.004_root_ssh

# --- Rare, high-signal socket use (Neo23x0 upstream, 2026-05) --------------
## Unlike the per-connection block below, these are RARE - they are not
## per-connection telemetry, so they stay in INTERNET too.
## AF_ALG (a0=38) = the KERNEL crypto API. Ransomware that encrypts via the
## kernel instead of a userspace crypto library leaves no library trace.
## a2=88 is the SOCKADDR_ALG bind; a1=279 is SOL_ALG on setsockopt.
## NOTE we deliberately DROP upstream's "-F auid>=1000 -F auid!=unset" scoping:
## that excludes daemons, and ransomware usually runs FROM a compromised
## service (auid=unset). The cost is a few boot-time events from cryptsetup /
## dm-crypt, which is a fair price for the indicator.
## AF_ALG is kernel >=2.6.38, so on el6 (2.6.32) these load and never fire.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S socket -F a0=38 -F success=1 -k T1486_af_alg
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S bind -F a2=88 -F success=1 -k T1486_af_alg
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S setsockopt -F a1=279 -F success=1 -k T1486_af_alg
## Raw sockets (AF_PACKET=17, SOCK_RAW=3) - sniffers and covert channels.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S socket -F a0=17 -F a1=3 -k T1040_raw_socket
## SO_ATTACH_FILTER (a1=SOL_SOCKET=1, a2=26) - a BPF filter attached to a
## socket, i.e. packet capture.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S setsockopt -F a1=1 -F a2=26 -k T1040_socket_bpf

# --- Special files, mounts, swap, network config ---------------------------
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S mknod -S mknodat -k T1546_specialfiles
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S mount -S umount2 -F auid!=-1 -k T1078_mount
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S swapon -S swapoff -F auid!=-1 -k T1078_swap
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S sethostname -S setdomainname -k T1082_hostname
-w /etc/hosts -p wa -k T1565_hosts
-w /etc/sysconfig/network -p wa -k T1565_network
-w /etc/sysconfig/network-scripts -p w -k T1565_network
-w /etc/network/ -p wa -k T1565_network
-a always,exit -F dir=/etc/NetworkManager/ -F perm=wa -k T1565_networkmanager
-w /etc/issue     -p wa -k T1082_issue
-w /etc/issue.net -p wa -k T1082_issue
-w /etc/hostname  -p wa -k T1082_hostname

# --- Anti-forensics + defence evasion (sigma: dead rules made fireable) -----
## Each of these is a documented BYPASS of the Neo23x0 config we forked - the
## repo CheraghiMilad/bypass-Neo23x0-auditd-config exists to defeat it - and
## SigmaHQ already ships the detection. All are near-zero frequency.
## syslog(2) actions: 4=SYSLOG_ACTION_READ_CLEAR, 5=CLEAR, 6=CONSOLE_OFF.
## Wiping or muting the kernel ring buffer to destroy evidence.
## sigma: lnx_auditd_clean_disable_dmesg_via_syslog.yml
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S syslog -F a0=4 -k T1070.002_dmesg_clear
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S syslog -F a0=5 -k T1070.002_dmesg_clear
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S syslog -F a0=6 -k T1562.006_dmesg_off
## personality(ADDR_NO_RANDOMIZE) - disabling ASLR to make an exploit reliable.
## sigma: lnx_auditd_disable_aslr_protection.yml
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S personality -F a0=0x40000 -k T1055.009_aslr_off
## Our own transport's config - we forward via rsyslog, so tampering here
## blinds the pipeline. sigma: lnx_auditd_logging_config_change.yml
-w /etc/rsyslog.conf -p wa -k T1562.006_logging
-w /etc/rsyslog.d/   -p wa -k T1562.006_logging
## setenforce(8) - putting SELinux in permissive mode. setenforce 0 writes
## /sys/fs/selinux/enforce, which -w /etc/selinux/ cannot see, so the binary
## itself has to be watched. No baseline in our lineage covers this.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -F path=/usr/sbin/setenforce -F perm=x -k T1562.001_selinux
## systemd-run re-parents a payload to PID 1, detaching it from the caller's
## session and auid - evasion of the auditing itself. Upstream STIG and OSPP
## both single it out: "It is not audit aware, specifically watch it".
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -F path=/usr/bin/systemd-run -F perm=x -F auid!=unset -k T1548_systemd_run

# --- Successful outbound connections (network telemetry) -------------------
## High volume on egress-heavy hosts - the INTERNET profile drops this block.
## DUAL-KEYED: ours (ATT&CK) + Sigma's. A rule may carry more than one -k
## (auditctl.8) - it is ONE rule, one record, no extra cost, searchable both
## ways. SigmaHQ hard-codes the upstream key name: its
## network_service_scanning rule matches key='network_connect_4' literally.
## Keep both keys - do not tidy either away.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S connect -F a2=16 -F success=1 -k T1071_connect4 -k network_connect_4
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S connect -F a2=28 -F success=1 -k T1071_connect6
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S socket -F a0=2  -k T1011_socket
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S socket -F a0=10 -k T1011_socket

# --- Indicator removal / anti-forensics ------------------------------------
-w /usr/bin/shred -p x -k T1070.004_shred
-w /usr/bin/wipe  -p x -k T1070.004_wipe
-w /usr/bin/scrub -p x -k T1070.004_scrub
-w /usr/bin/dd    -p x -k T1070.004_dd
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S rmdir -S unlink -S unlinkat -S rename -S renameat -F auid>=1000 -F auid!=-1 -k T1070.004_filedel
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S rmdir -S unlink -S unlinkat -S rename -S renameat -F auid=0 -k T1070.004_filedel_root

# --- Deobfuscation / decode ------------------------------------------------
-w /usr/bin/certutil -p x -k T1140_certutil
-w /usr/bin/unzip   -p x -k T1140_unzip
-w /usr/bin/base64  -p x -k T1140_base64 -k susp_activity
-w /usr/bin/openssl -p x -k T1140_openssl

# --- Ingress tools / remote access -----------------------------------------
## DUAL-KEYED with Sigma's susp_activity. A rule may carry more than one -k
## (auditctl.8) - one rule, one record, no extra cost. SigmaHQ's
## susp_c2_commands rule matches key='susp_activity' literally (the upstream
## key name), so a TID-only key leaves it dead. Keep both keys on these.
-w /usr/bin/wget  -p x -k T1105_wget -k susp_activity
-w /usr/bin/curl  -p x -k T1105_curl -k susp_activity
-w /usr/bin/ftp   -p x -k T1105_ftp
-w /usr/bin/scp   -p x -k T1105_scp
-w /usr/bin/sftp  -p x -k T1105_sftp
-w /usr/bin/ssh   -p x -k T1021.004_ssh -k susp_activity
-w /usr/bin/socat -p x -k T1105_socat
-w /bin/nc        -p x -k T1105_nc -k susp_activity
-w /bin/nc.openbsd     -p x -k T1105_nc -k susp_activity
-w /bin/nc.traditional -p x -k T1105_nc -k susp_activity
-w /usr/bin/ncat  -p x -k T1105_ncat -k susp_activity
-w /usr/bin/rdesktop  -p x -k T1219_rat
-w /usr/bin/xfreerdp  -p x -k T1219_rat -k susp_activity
-w /usr/bin/teamviewer -p x -k T1219_rat

# --- Mail transport --------------------------------------------------------
-w /etc/aliases  -p wa -k T1114_mail
-w /etc/postfix/ -p wa -k T1114_mail
-w /etc/exim4/   -p wa -k T1114_mail

# --- Stunnel (customer tunnel transport - customer sites) ---------------
-w /usr/sbin/stunnel -p x -k T1573.002_stunnel
-w /usr/bin/stunnel  -p x -k T1573.002_stunnel

# --- MAC (SELinux) and power state -----------------------------------------
-w /etc/selinux/ -p wa -k T1562.001_selinux
## AppArmor is the Ubuntu/Debian MAC - the SELinux watches miss it entirely, so
## editing a profile or unloading one via apparmor_parser is a whole-distro
## T1562.001 blind spot without these.
-w /etc/apparmor    -p wa -k T1562.001_apparmor
-w /etc/apparmor.d  -p wa -k T1562.001_apparmor
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -F path=/sbin/apparmor_parser -F perm=x -k T1562.001_apparmor
-w /sbin/shutdown -p x -k T1529_power
-w /sbin/poweroff -p x -k T1529_power
-w /sbin/reboot   -p x -k T1529_power
-w /sbin/halt     -p x -k T1529_power

# --- Container runtimes / escape (T1610 / T1611) ---------------------------
-w /usr/bin/docker  -p x -k T1610_docker
-w /usr/bin/dockerd -p x -k T1610_docker
-w /var/run/docker.sock -p rwa -k T1610_docker_sock
-w /etc/docker -p wa -k T1610_docker_config
-w /usr/bin/podman     -p x -k T1610_podman
-w /usr/bin/containerd -p x -k T1610_containerd
-w /usr/bin/runc       -p x -k T1610_runc
-w /usr/bin/crictl     -p x -k T1610_crictl
-w /usr/bin/kubelet    -p x -k T1610_kubelet

# --- Software / package management -----------------------------------------
-w /usr/bin/rpm -p x -k T1072_pkg
-w /usr/bin/yum -p x -k T1072_pkg
-w /usr/bin/dnf -p x -k T1072_pkg
-w /usr/bin/zypper -k T1072_pkg
-w /sbin/yast  -p x -k T1072_pkg
-w /sbin/yast2 -p x -k T1072_pkg
-w /usr/bin/dpkg -p x -k T1072_pkg
-w /usr/bin/apt-get -p x -k T1072_pkg
-w /usr/bin/apt-add-repository -p x -k T1072_pkg
-w /usr/bin/aptitude -p x -k T1072_pkg
-w /usr/bin/snap -p x -k T1072_pkg
-w /usr/bin/pip  -p x -k T1072_pkg
-w /usr/bin/pip3 -p x -k T1072_pkg
-w /usr/bin/npm  -p x -k T1072_pkg
-w /usr/bin/gem  -p x -k T1072_pkg
-w /usr/bin/cpan -p x -k T1072_pkg
-w /usr/bin/luarocks -p x -k T1072_pkg

# ===========================================================================
# HIGH-only additional coverage (heavier volume - not in MEDIUM)
# ===========================================================================

# --- Interpreter / shell execution (T1059) ---------------------------------
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F path=/usr/bin/bash   -F perm=x -F auid=0 -k T1059.004_root_shell
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F path=/bin/bash       -F perm=x -F auid=0 -k T1059.004_root_shell
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F path=/usr/bin/python3 -F perm=x -k T1059.006_python
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F path=/usr/bin/python  -F perm=x -k T1059.006_python
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F path=/usr/bin/perl    -F perm=x -k T1059.006_perl

# --- Privilege-context execution + setuid/setgid family (T1548.001) --------
## MUST keep -F auid!=unset. Daemons have auid=unset (4294967295) and uid=0,
## so "auid!=uid" is TRUE for EVERY daemon execve - without this guard the
## rule fires on every process spawn by every service on the box.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -C auid!=uid -F auid!=unset -k T1548.001_priv_ctx_exec
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S setuid -S setgid -S setreuid -S setregid -F auid!=-1 -k T1548.001_setid
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S setuid -S setgid -S setreuid -S setregid -F exit=-EPERM -k T1548.001_setid_fail

# --- Failed access to critical trees (T1068 exploitation attempts) ---------
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S open -S openat -F dir=/etc -F success=0 -F auid>=1000 -F auid!=-1 -k T1068_denied
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S open -S openat -F dir=/root -F success=0 -F auid>=1000 -F auid!=-1 -k T1068_denied

# --- IPC (T1559) -----------------------------------------------------------
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S msgctl -S msgget -k T1559_ipc
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S semctl -S semget -S semop -S semtimedop -k T1559_ipc
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S shmctl -S shmget -k T1559_ipc

# --- Recon toolset (T1046 / T1049 / T1057 / T1082 / T1518) ------------------
-w /usr/bin/whoami   -p x -k T1033_recon
-w /usr/bin/id       -p x -k T1033_recon
-w /bin/hostname     -p x -k T1082_recon
-w /bin/uname        -p x -k T1082_recon
## Dual-keyed with Sigma's susp_activity, same reason as the ingress block
## above - the upstream key name is what SigmaHQ matches on.
-w /usr/bin/nmap     -p x -k T1046_recon -k susp_activity
-w /usr/bin/ss       -p x -k T1049_recon -k susp_activity
-w /usr/bin/netstat  -p x -k T1049_recon -k susp_activity
-w /usr/sbin/tcpdump -p x -k T1049_recon
-w /usr/sbin/traceroute -p x -k T1049_recon
-w /usr/bin/tshark   -p x -k T1049_recon
-w /usr/bin/wireshark -p x -k T1049_recon
-w /sbin/iptables    -p x -k T1518.001_recon
-w /usr/sbin/nft     -p x -k T1518.001_recon
-w /sbin/ifconfig    -p x -k T1518.001_recon
-w /usr/bin/getenforce -p x -k T1518.001_recon
-w /usr/bin/showmount  -p x -k T1135_recon
-w /usr/sbin/exportfs  -p x -k T1135_recon
-w /etc/exports -p wa -k T1135_recon
-w /etc/fstab   -p wa -k T1135_recon

# --- Binaries SigmaHQ matches on (sigma: makes dead rules fireable) -------
## Each emits an EXECVE record with argv when executed, which is what Sigma's
## type:EXECVE rules match. A general "-S execve" would cover these too, at the
## CPU cost the perf reviews exist to avoid - these are the targeted alternative.
-w /usr/bin/chattr   -p x -k T1222.002_chattr
-w /usr/sbin/getcap  -p x -k T1548_getcap
-w /usr/bin/steghide -p x -k T1027.003_steg
-w /usr/bin/last     -p x -k T1033_recon

# --- Data staging / local copy (T1005 / T1074 / T1560) ---------------------
-w /usr/bin/cp   -p x -k T1074_stage
-w /usr/bin/mv   -p x -k T1074_stage
-w /usr/bin/tar  -p x -k T1560_archive
-w /usr/bin/gzip -p x -k T1560_archive
-w /usr/bin/zip  -p x -k T1560_archive
-w /usr/bin/bzip2 -p x -k T1560_archive
-w /usr/bin/zstd -p x -k T1560_archive
-w /usr/bin/xz   -p x -k T1560_archive

# --- Session / login records (T1078 / T1136) -------------------------------
-w /var/run/utmp -p wa -k T1078.001_session
-w /var/log/wtmp -p wa -k T1078.001_session
-w /var/log/btmp -p wa -k T1078.001_session
-w /var/log/faillog -p wa -k T1078.001_login
-w /var/log/lastlog -p wa -k T1078.001_login
-w /var/log/tallylog -p wa -k T1078.001_login
-w /var/log/secure -p wa -k T1078.001_login

# --- PAM (T1556) -----------------------------------------------------------
-w /etc/pam.d/ -p wa -k T1556_pam
## Legacy single-file PAM config - the /etc/pam.d/ directory form is watched
## above; older/minimal systems still use the monolithic /etc/pam.conf.
-w /etc/pam.conf -p wa -k T1556_pam
## nsswitch.conf selects the passwd/group/shadow resolution backend - repointing
## it is an auth-bypass primitive (T1556). Rare to change.
-w /etc/nsswitch.conf -p wa -k T1556_nsswitch
-w /etc/security/limits.conf -p wa -k T1556_pam
-w /etc/security/limits.d -p wa -k T1556_pam
-w /etc/security/pam_env.conf -p wa -k T1556_pam

# --- Pre-OS boot (T1542) ---------------------------------------------------
-w /boot/grub2/grub.cfg -p wa -k T1542.003_boot
-w /boot/grub/grub.cfg -p wa -k T1542.003_boot

# --- Third-party agents / config-mgmt (customer + infra) -------------------
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S open -S openat -F dir=/opt/BESClient -F success=0 -k T1072_besclient
-w /var/opt/BESClient/ -p wa -k T1072_besclient
-w /etc/chef -p wa -k T1072_chef
-w /etc/salt -p wa -k T1072_salt
-w /etc/puppet/ssl -p wa -k T1072_puppet

# --- Make the configuration immutable (MUST be last) -----------------------
-e 2
